-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Enclosed Inactive Blocks:** Inactive blocks enclosed by active blocks are not excluded from the search range by the bounding geometry (object-order skip) [S1]. Samples in these blocks are skipped on a per-sample basis (image-order skip) early in the ray-casting loop [S1].
-*   **Isovalue Changes:** The active status of blocks must be recomputed whenever the isovalue changes, as this affects the min-max culling [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Enclosed Inactive Blocks:** Inactive blocks enclosed by active blocks are not excluded from the search range $[t_{start}, t_{exit}]$ by the bounding geometry. Samples in these blocks are skipped on a per-sample basis early in the ray-casting loop [S1].
+- **Performance Overhead:** Inactive blocks enclosed by active blocks result in per-sample checks, which adds overhead compared to fully excluded regions [S1].
+- **Isovalue Changes:** When the isovalue changes, the active status of blocks must be recomputed, and the bounding geometry updated [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Early Shader Termination](969-early-shader-termination.md)
+Early Shader Termination is an optimization technique that discards the execution of complex decoding shaders for fragments likely to represent empty space by using an early z-test in a two-pass rendering approach. It builds on [Object-Order Empty Space Skipping](865-object-order-empty-space-skipping.md) by adding a lightweight pre-check to avoid expensive shader execution in empty regions [G]. Compared to rendering uncompressed data, this approach initially perceives a loss in performance of a factor of 2-3 due to complex shaders and texture indirections; however, in empty regions, it results in a considerable speed-up because only one texture fetch is needed to check the mean.
+
+### [Rasterizing Bounding Geometry for Empty Space Skipping](5141-rasterizing-bounding-geometry-for-empty-space-skipping.md)
+Rasterizing Bounding Geometry for Empty Space Skipping is a volume rendering optimization technique that uses hardware-accelerated rasterization to determine the start and end positions for ray marching, thereby skipping empty space. It solves the problem of expensive tree traversal on the GPU by replacing hierarchical data structure lookups with fragment shader depth testing. It sets itself apart from [Min-Max Block Culling](8368-min-max-block-culling.md) by using explicit geometric primitives (quads/boxes) and depth buffers rather than scalar min-max values, and from [Early Shader Termination](969-early-shader-termination.md) by performing culling in a dedicated geometry pass rather than within the ray-marching loop.
+
+### [Min-Max Block Culling](8368-min-max-block-culling.md)
+Min-Max Block Culling is an object-order empty space skipping technique that reduces the number of volume samples by culling transparent or irrelevant regions before raycasting begins. It differs from its siblings by performing culling during the rasterization setup phase using precomputed block statistics, rather than during ray traversal or via bounding geometry rasterization. Unlike [Early Shader Termination](969-early-shader-termination.md), which stops rays dynamically during compositing, this method prevents rays from entering empty blocks entirely. Unlike [Rasterizing Bounding Geometry for Empty Space Skipping](5141-rasterizing-bounding-geometry-for-empty-space-skipping.md), it relies on scalar block bounds rather than polygonal approximations.
+
 ### Overlapped Min-Max Block
-A data structure for empty-space skipping in pre-integrated volume rendering [S3]. It modifies the region covered by each block so that each block covers some region of which thickness is at least the sampling distance [S3]. By overlapping the region of each block, a line segment formed by two sampling points can be tested for transparency using only one block, avoiding the overhead of determining which of multiple intersecting blocks are transparent [S3]. Unlike previous methods where a line segment may intersect at most three blocks and requires testing all intersecting blocks (creating overhead to determine cases like Fig. 1a vs 1b), this method tests only one block due to the overlapping region design [S3].
+A data structure for empty-space skipping in pre-integrated volume rendering. It modifies the region covered by each block so that each block covers some region of which thickness is at least the sampling distance. By overlapping the region of each block, a line segment formed by two sampling points can be tested for transparency using only one block, avoiding the overhead of determining which of multiple intersecting blocks are transparent [S3].
+
+- **Definition:** Each block covers a region of thickness at least the sampling distance, creating overlaps between adjacent blocks [S3].
+- **Steps:**
+  1. Modify the region covered by each block such that each block covers a region of thickness at least the sampling distance, creating overlaps between adjacent blocks [S3].
+  2. To skip a line segment formed by two sampling points, test only the single block that contains the line segment (or overlaps it sufficiently) to determine if it is transparent. If the block is transparent, skip the sampling process for that segment [S3].
+- **Comparison:** Unlike previous methods where a line segment may intersect at most three blocks and requires testing all intersecting blocks (creating overhead to determine cases like Fig. 1a vs 1b), this method tests only one block due to the overlapping region design [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources [G]. Rendering cost is proportional to the number of active blocks and samples within them [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires additional memory for the min-max block structure, which is $O(N/B^3)$, significantly smaller than the volume itself [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Most empty space is skipped with no cost using modified ray segments $[t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{exit}}]$ [S1]. Only for a small number of samples empty space has to be skipped on a sample-by-sample basis [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Inactive blocks enclosed by active blocks are not excluded by the bounding geometry, requiring per-sample checks [S1]. The bounding geometry must be updated or recomputed if the isovalue changes [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for GPU-based ray-casting where the volume fits in memory or can be managed with bricks, and where the isovalue may change interactively [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** The object-order skipping phase is $O(1)$ per pixel for depth range computation (via rasterization) and $O(N)$ for ray casting, where $N$ is the number of samples in the active range. The per-sample check for image-order skipping is $O(1)$.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(V/B^3)$ for the min-max block structure, where $V$ is the volume size and $B$ is the block size [S1].
+- **Measured Performance:** The method reduces overhead by skipping most empty space with no cost using modified ray segments. Only a small number of samples require per-sample skipping, accelerated via adaptive sampling [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Reduces the number of volume samples significantly for sparse volumes. Simple to implement with standard GPU rasterization features.
+- **Limitations:** Inactive blocks enclosed by active blocks are not excluded from the search range, leading to per-sample checks. Performance may degrade if the volume is dense or if the block size is not optimal [S1].
+- **When to Use:** Suitable for sparse volumes with clear isosurfaces or transparent regions. Effective when combined with adaptive sampling [S1].
+- **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for dense volumes where most blocks are active, as the overhead of per-sample checks may outweigh the benefits [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from real-time ray-casting techniques for discrete isosurfaces, this method was introduced to overcome limitations of earlier multi-pass approaches [S1]. It allows for a single rendering pass with front-to-back looping in the fragment shader, employing both object-order and image-order empty space skipping [S1]. The technique is particularly effective for interactive applications where the isovalue needs to be adjusted in real-time [S1].
+Object-Order Empty Space Skipping was introduced in the context of GPU-based ray-casting for real-time volume rendering [S1]. It builds on the idea of using block min-max values to cull inactive regions, reducing the overhead of empty space skipping [S1]. The method has been used in various implementations of real-time volume rendering, particularly for isosurface rendering [S1].
 
